def P(t): return "".join(f"<p>{x.strip()}</p>" for x in t.split("||"))
body = []

INTRO=P('A crisp, sunny Monday that climbs only to about 50 behind Sunday’s front starts a cold, short Thanksgiving week, with the first hard freeze of the season likely at Tuesday’s dawn, a damp Wednesday and a bright, mild Thanksgiving Day. Schools are out, trash days shift, and the community Thanksgiving meal is set for Thursday at St. Bernards. Arkansas’ federal data-center hearing resumes this morning; Rep. Rick Crawford is pressing for answers in the Nvidia affair; and the world climate talks in Turkey broke up without a deal.')
ZONE='<table class="mk"><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Archery</th><th>Youth gun</th><th>Modern gun</th><th>Muzzleloader</th></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14 – Dec. 6; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Oct. 17-25; Dec. 12-14, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14-22; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Closed</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14 – Dec. 6; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Oct. 17-25; Dec. 12-14, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14 – Dec. 6; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Oct. 17-25; Dec. 12-14, 2026</td></tr></table>'
body.append('<h1>The Morning Letter</h1><div class="sub">With the compliments of the Editor, to Jonesboro, Brookland &amp; neighbors</div>')
body.append('<div class="date">Monday, the Twenty-Third of November, 2026</div><div class="orn">&#10086;</div>')
body.append('<p>Good morning,</p>')
body.append(INTRO)
body.append('<h2>The Day Ahead</h2>'); body.append(hourly); body.append(grid)
body.append(P('The front that came through Sunday has left a crisp, honest November morning behind it: about 38 degrees at six o’clock under a clear sky, with a bank of cloud drifting over around nine, when it is near 41. The sun wins out by noon, and the afternoon is bright and dry, about 47 at midday and 49 or 50 by three, with a steady breeze of 12 or 13 miles an hour that makes it seem cooler than the thermometer says. There is no rain in the forecast at all today. After sunset the air cools quickly, to about 42 by six and 38 by nine.||Tuesday brings the coldest dawn of the season so far, near 31, and a gray day under thick cloud that tops out about 45. Wednesday, the great travel day, is raw and damp, with clouds all day, a high near 43 and about a three-in-ten chance of rain, mostly light. Thanksgiving Day itself looks kind: a cloudy start near 40 giving way to sunshine and about 51 in the afternoon. Friday is sunny and near 55, and the weekend warms to about 63 on Saturday and 67 on Sunday, fine weather for the ball games and the drive home.'))
body.append('<h2>The Almanac</h2>')
body.append(f'''<table class="alm"><tr><td>First light</td><td>{alm['first_light']}</td><td>Sunrise</td><td>{alm['sunrise']}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solar noon</td><td>{alm['noon']}</td><td>Sunset</td><td>{alm['sunset']}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Last light</td><td>{alm['last_light']}</td><td>Length of day</td><td>{alm['day_length']}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Change from yesterday</td><td>{alm['change'].replace('-','&minus;')}</td><td>Moon</td><td>{alm['moon']}, {alm['illum']}% lit</td></tr></table>''')
body.append(P('The sun rises at 6:47 and sets at 4:50, giving ten hours and three minutes of daylight, about a minute and 23 seconds less than Sunday’s. The moon is 95 percent lit and will be full around Tuesday, the 24th, the last full moon before winter begins; with clear skies tonight it will light the yards almost like a lantern. On this date in 1804, Franklin Pierce, the 14th president, was born in Hillsborough, New Hampshire.'))
body.append(P('<i>In field and garden:</i> Tuesday’s dawn near 31 may be the first true freeze of the season for many yards around Jonesboro and Brookland, so this afternoon is the time to bring in potted ferns and geraniums, unhook garden hoses and wrap any outdoor faucets and well pumps that sit in the open. Pecans are falling in the yards now, and a walk under the trees this afternoon should fill a bucket for the Thursday pie; let them dry in a cool place a week or two before cracking if you can wait. In the kitchen, if your turkey is still frozen, the old rule from the Department of Agriculture is about one day in the refrigerator for every four to five pounds, so a big bird should go in the refrigerator today. In the woods the oaks are dropping the last of their leaves, and squirrels are busy burying acorns where the fallen leaves will cover them.'))
body.append('<p><i>Weather lore, not a forecast:</i> an old country saying holds, “If there’s ice in November that will bear a duck, there’ll be nothing after but sludge and muck.” Tuesday’s freeze will not make ice that thick, so by the old reckoning the winter has not yet shown its hand.</p>')

body.append('<h2>Of Local Interest</h2>')
body.append(P('It was a quiet weekend at home, as the weekend before Thanksgiving usually is, and most of the news is of the holiday sort.'))
body.append('<h3>Schools out, trash days shifted for Thanksgiving week</h3>')
body.append(P('Jonesboro Public Schools are out all week, today through Friday, for the Thanksgiving holiday. City trash pickup in Jonesboro runs as usual Monday through Wednesday, but the city’s Sanitation Department is closed Thursday and Friday; under the city’s 2026 collection calendar, the Thursday route will be picked up next Monday, Nov. 30, and the Friday route on Tuesday, Dec. 1. Check the city’s calendar if your days fall later in the week.||Brookland city offices close Thursday. The Brookland Planning Commission, which reviews building and zoning requests for the city, next meets Tuesday, Dec. 1, at 7 p.m.; items for its agenda are due by noon Wednesday. In Jonesboro, the City Council’s Finance and Administration Committee, which reviews spending and budget matters before they go to the full council, meets Tuesday at 4 p.m.'))
body.append('<h3>NEA Baptist lights its tree and gathers gifts for children</h3>')
body.append(P('NEA Baptist Memorial Hospital, the Jonesboro hospital on East Johnson Avenue, held its annual Christmas tree lighting in the hospital lobby Sunday evening, with carols, refreshments and visitors dressed as Santa and as Elsa from the film <i>Frozen</i> to greet the children. Admission was free, and guests were asked to bring new socks and underwear for Children’s Homes, a faith-based ministry that provides foster care and residential care for children. Ty Jones, the hospital’s marketing director, said the evening is a way to “give back to the community,” and that it also lets patients and their families come out of their rooms and enjoy the season during a hard time.'))
body.append('<h3>A dry year, but the Christmas trees held up</h3>')
body.append(P('Months of drought have left rivers across Northeast Arkansas low or dry in places, but Crow’s Nest Christmas Tree Farm, on Crowley’s Ridge in Greene County, expects a normal season. Owner Kimberly Dale, who began growing trees about ten years ago, said her Virginia pines mostly came through, apart from a few stressed trees and some early yellowing, and that rain in the coming weeks would help. Farms that depend on irrigation may have a harder year, she said, and hers plans to irrigate in future dry years if it must. The farm’s last day of store hours this season is Sunday, Dec. 6.'))
body.append('<h3>Winter markets keep a paycheck coming for small farmers</h3>')
body.append(P('Shoppers filled the Judd Hill Christmas Market in Jonesboro on Saturday, and for the local growers who sell there, the holiday markets bring in money after the regular farmers market season ends. “The winter markets definitely help because I don’t really have anywhere to set up anymore now,” said Andrew Allen, who manages Sunshine Farms; he said the markets also let him meet customers and show them where their food comes from. The Judd Hill Farmers Market has one more winter market this year, Saturday, Dec. 19, from 9 a.m. to 2 p.m. at 3350 Aggie Road.'))
body.append('<h3>In brief</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>A new teacher at the medical school.</b> Dr. William Tedjo, a doctor of osteopathic medicine trained in osteopathic manipulative medicine, a hands-on treatment of muscles and joints, has joined the New York Institute of Technology College of Osteopathic Medicine at Arkansas State, the medical school on the Jonesboro campus, as an assistant professor. Patients can make appointments with him through the school’s clinic.'))
body.append('<h3>Around town</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Today, Monday, Nov. 23.</b> Bluegrass Monday with the Williamson Branch, presented by KASU, the A-State public radio station, at the Collins Theatre, 120 W. Emerson St., Paragould; jam session at 5 p.m. next door, show at 7 ($10, cash only at the door, or at kasu.org; 18 and under free). <i>The Nutcracker</i> by the Foundation of Arts at the Forum Theater, 115 E. Monroe Ave., 7 p.m. tonight and Tuesday, closing night ($22 to $26; 870-935-2726).||<b>Tuesday, Nov. 24.</b> Last day to sign up for home delivery of a free Jonesboro Community Thanksgiving Meal (Jonesboro addresses only, up to six meals per address, while the 1,700 delivery spots last). Santa’s North Pole opens at 4502 Access Road and runs through Christmas Eve ($8; hours vary).||<b>Thanksgiving Day, Thursday, Nov. 26.</b> DAV Chapter 26 Turkey Trot 5K, 7 a.m. (registration at 6), from the military science building at 1921 Aggie Road ($40). The 30th Jonesboro Community Thanksgiving Meal, 10 to 11:30 a.m. or until the food runs out, St. Bernards Auditorium, 505 E. Washington Ave. (free; coats and blankets will also be given out; volunteers welcome at 9:30, no sign-up needed; organizers ask for desserts that need no refrigeration, in disposable containers). Christmas at the Park, the drive-through lights at Joe Mack Campbell Park, 600 Harry Drive, opens and runs through Dec. 24, 5:30 to 8:30 p.m. Sunday through Thursday and to 9:30 on weekends.||<b>Looking ahead.</b> Paragould Christmas parade, Dec. 1, 7 p.m.; Jonesboro Christmas Parade on Main Street, Dec. 4, 7 p.m.; Downtown JoyFest, Dec. 5, 3 to 11 p.m.; and the 25th Brookland Christmas Parade, Sunday, Dec. 6, at 2 p.m., lining up at Brookland High School (no registration needed; candy may be thrown; city hall, 870-935-0538).'))
body.append('<h3>Our Neighbors Farther Afield</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Lepanto.</b> Dozens of volunteers gathered at First Missionary Baptist Church on Saturday morning to bag groceries for the 10th annual Thanksgiving turkey drive put on by Malik Monk, the Lepanto native who plays in the NBA for the Sacramento Kings. Organizers said the drive would help about 450 families in Lepanto, Tyronza, Marked Tree and nearby towns.||<b>Across the river.</b> Two small earthquakes shook the New Madrid fault zone on Sunday: a magnitude 3.1 at about 9:04 a.m. about 7 miles west of Dyersburg, Tenn., and a 2.1 at 12:47 p.m. about 5 miles southwest of Tiptonville, Tenn., according to the U.S. Geological Survey. A few people reported feeling the first one; no damage was reported.'))

body.append('<div style="break-inside:avoid;page-break-inside:avoid"><h2>From the Police Reports</h2><p class="note">Jonesboro Police incident reports. Charges are allegations. No names are printed.</p><p>As of early Monday the department had posted no new incident reports since Friday’s batch, which ran in Saturday’s letter. The weekend’s reports will appear here once they are posted.</p>')
body.append('<p class="note" style="break-after:avoid;margin-bottom:2pt">At the county jails</p><p>The Craighead County jail roster listed 321 people held early Monday, with 24 booked in the last 48 hours. Among the newest bookings listed first, impaired-driving charges were the most common, three of them from Saturday evening to late Sunday, along with a theft charge, a marijuana-possession charge, a domestic-battery charge, a charge of leaving the scene of a crash and a charge of aggravated assault on a health-care worker. In Pocahontas, the Randolph County Sheriff’s Office listed 61 people held, with one new booking in the last 48 hours, on license charges, and one person released; the newest bookings there were mostly on license, drug, warrant and release-violation charges.</p></div>')

body.append('<h2>From the State</h2>')
body.append('<h3>Data center hearing resumes this morning in federal court</h3>')
body.append(P('A federal hearing over AVAIO Digital’s planned $6 billion data center near Wrightsville, in southern Pulaski County, resumes at 8:30 a.m. today in Little Rock before U.S. District Judge Lee Rudofsky. The company is asking for a preliminary injunction, a court order that would keep Pulaski County’s new data-center ordinance from being enforced while its lawsuit goes forward. The Quorum Court, the county’s elected governing board, passed the ordinance in October over the veto of County Judge Barry Hyde.||On Friday the judge refused to sign an agreement that AVAIO and lawyers for Judge Hyde drafted during a recess, which would have declared that the ordinance “does not apply” to the company’s site plan; he said it “all looks a little too perfect” and that he would not sign anything binding future county officials. The two sides may still settle on their own; otherwise he will rule on the injunction. County Judge-elect Wendell Griffen, who takes office Jan. 1, has said he expects to enforce the ordinance within weeks.'))
body.append('<h3>Supreme Court ruling revives old child sex-abuse lawsuits</h3>')
body.append(P('Lawsuits against a former Pulaski County pediatrician and the owners of a former youth home are moving forward again after the Arkansas Supreme Court ruled 3-2 this month that the Legislature could revive expired claims of childhood sexual abuse. The 2021 Justice for Vulnerable Victims of Sexual Abuse Act opened a two-year “look-back window” for such suits, and the Nov. 5 ruling upheld it, overturning some 180 years of state precedent; Chief Justice Karen Baker and Justice Shawn Womack dissented.||In one case, four plaintiffs accuse former pediatrician and camp counselor James Nesmith of abusing them between 1995 and 2007; it returns to Pulaski County Circuit Court, where their lawyers plan to ask for a trial date. In the other, 16 plaintiffs sued the owners of the former Lord’s Ranch in Warm Springs, Ted and Shirley Suhl, and a former counselor; a motion asks a federal judge to reinstate plaintiffs he had dismissed, and the defendants must answer by Dec. 2. The accusations are allegations and have not been proved in court. Because the window has closed, few new suits are expected.'))
body.append('<h3>Bar exam scandal leaves a question mark on some lawyers’ records</h3>')
body.append(P('Some lawyers who passed the Arkansas bar exam during the years under investigation are finding an asterisk on their files with the National Conference of Bar Examiners, the national group that keeps applicants’ exam records; what the mark means has not been explained, and it does not by itself show that anyone’s score was changed. Nancie Givens, who ran the Arkansas Supreme Court office that gives the exam until she resigned in February, is accused by prosecutors of altering results for at least 64 people between July 2020 and July 2025, moving points between applicants. She faces 65 felony counts of tampering with a public record and has pleaded not guilty.||Six of the seven justices have said that most applicants who were told they passed did earn passing scores and that affected examinees will be told once their status is confirmed. No motive has been given, and authorities have not said whether any applicant knew of the changes.'))
body.append('<h3>A look back at the election: gains, but no top office</h3>')
body.append(P('An analysis published this morning of the Nov. 3 election finds that Black candidates made gains in Arkansas but did not win a federal or top statewide office; Arkansas remains the only former Confederate state never to have elected a Black member of Congress. In the 2nd Congressional District, which covers central Arkansas, Democrat Chris Jones, a pastor and nuclear engineer, lost to Republican Rep. French Hill by less than a percentage point, the closest a Democrat has come to a federal win in the state in 16 years. State Sen. Fred Love, the Democratic nominee for governor, lost to Gov. Sarah Huckabee Sanders by double digits.||Wendell Griffen became the first Black person elected Pulaski County judge, and Democrats gained 10 seats in the state House, enough to end the Republicans’ three-fourths supermajority, the 75 votes needed to pass most budget bills, when the Legislature meets in January.'))
body.append('<h3>Thanksgiving travel: no lane closures on state highways</h3>')
body.append(P('Wednesday is expected to be the busiest day on Arkansas highways this week, and the state Department of Transportation plans no lane closures from Wednesday morning through next Monday. At Little Rock’s airport, federal security screeners expect about 47,600 passengers between Nov. 20 and Dec. 1, with the Sunday after Thanksgiving the busiest day. Gasoline is cheaper than it was a week ago (see Markets &amp; the Farm).'))

body.append('<h2>From the Nation</h2>')
body.append('<h3>Army says it will detonate Iowa explosives Wednesday despite a court pause</h3>')
body.append(P('The Army says it will carry out a controlled detonation of degraded lead azide, a highly sensitive explosive used in detonators, at the Iowa Army Ammunition Plant near Middletown, in southeastern Iowa, on Wednesday, even though a federal appeals court has paused the permit that allowed it. In a filing late Saturday with the 8th U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals in St. Louis, the Justice Department said the Army would rely on an emergency exception in federal environmental law that does not depend on the permit, because “the Army must perform a controlled detonation to protect the public from the more significant threat from an uncontrolled explosion.” The material, in 68 barrels found in March, has crystallized, and officials say it could go off on its own once temperatures fall below 30 degrees.||Two environmental groups, the Iowa Environmental Council and the Sierra Club, have asked the court to stop it, questioning whether safer methods were fully considered; their lawyers say the government is trying to get around the court. Residents nearby have been told to stay indoors with windows shut, bring pets and livestock inside and clean outdoor surfaces afterward, because the blast could scatter lead-bearing dust, and some of Middletown’s roughly 400 residents have been advised to leave. The court had not ruled on the groups’ request as of the latest reports.'))
body.append('<h3>Report ties woman in Nvidia scandal to Chinese spy agency; Rep. Crawford presses for answers</h3>')
body.append(P('The New York Times reported Sunday that Alice Huang, the woman at the center of the scandal that led Nvidia, the leading maker of artificial-intelligence chips, to remove co-founder Jensen Huang as chief executive, was in contact with an official of China’s Ministry of State Security, its main spy agency, from spring 2025 until this fall, while the two were romantically involved; ABC News said its own sources confirmed the account. The two are not related. It is not known whether she passed any inside information to China; she has denied similar allegations before, and no charges have been filed against anyone. She formerly ran Megaspeed, a Singapore company federal investigators have examined since 2024 over claims it helped Nvidia chips reach China in violation of U.S. export rules.||On Fox News Sunday, Rep. Rick Crawford, the Jonesboro Republican who represents Arkansas’ 1st District and chairs the House Intelligence Committee, said he and the committee’s senior Democrat, Rep. Jim Himes of Connecticut, wrote to Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick nine days ago asking whether advanced chips reached China, and “we haven’t heard back.” He called a subpoena “premature” and said Nvidia’s leaders are expected at a closed-door congressional interview Dec. 1. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said on CBS that “we do need answers” from the company about whether it had “a culture of compliance.”'))
body.append('<h3>Rep. Virginia Foxx concedes North Carolina seat after recount</h3>')
body.append(P('Rep. Virginia Foxx, a Republican who has represented North Carolina’s 5th Congressional District for 11 terms, conceded to Democrat Chuck Hubbard on Sunday after a recount left him ahead by 330 votes; the machine recount finished Saturday gained her a single vote. Foxx said she called Mr. Hubbard to congratulate him, and he thanked her for “decades of service.” It was North Carolina’s last undecided House race from the Nov. 3 election, and it adds one more seat to the Democrats’ incoming House majority.'))
body.append('<h3>Senate Democrats spar over leadership; officials split on the outages</h3>')
body.append(P('Sen. Chris Van Hollen of Maryland, who is challenging Sen. Chuck Schumer of New York to be majority leader when Democrats take control of the Senate in January, said on NBC’s “Meet the Press” that his support is “north of 15” senators and that “momentum is in our direction”; Mr. Schumer says he has the votes to stay on, and published counts differ widely ahead of the secret-ballot vote on Dec. 1. Mr. Van Hollen called the filibuster, the rule that requires 60 votes to pass most bills, “an anti-democratic provision,” and President Trump wrote that it would be “very hard for me to work with Chuck Schumer” as majority leader.||On the same Sunday programs, officials differed on whether the recent string of government and corporate computer outages and hacks, including the breach of the FBI’s national crime database, are connected. Secretary of State Marco Rubio said, “We are not under systemic attack,” and former Homeland Security Secretary Jeh Johnson said the incidents are not “the product of one common bad actor,” while Republican Sen. John Kennedy of Louisiana said he had “no idea.” Congress must pass a spending bill by Dec. 11 to avoid a government shutdown.'))
body.append('<h3>In brief</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Concert shooting in California.</b> Nine people were wounded, one critically, when gunfire broke out Saturday evening at a private concert of about 150 people in Strathmore, a farm town of some 3,000 in California’s San Joaquin Valley; the Tulare County sheriff’s office said it “may be gang-related.” No arrests had been announced.||<b>Holiday weather and travel.</b> The coastal storm that soaked Florida last week weakened as it moved up the East Coast, and the chance that it becomes a named storm has faded; heavy rain was expected from New Jersey to southern Maine into this morning, and another wet system could reach the Interstate 95 corridor from Raleigh to New York by Thanksgiving Day. AAA expects about 81.5 million Americans to travel for the holiday, slightly fewer than last year, with Tuesday and Wednesday the busiest days on the road.'))
body.append('<h2>From Abroad</h2>')
body.append('<h3>Climate talks end without a deal for the first time since 2009</h3>')
body.append(P('The United Nations climate summit known as COP31, in Antalya, Turkey, ended Sunday without adopting its main agreement, after the European Union and others refused a final text that did not commit to moving away from fossil fuels. “I understand that there is no agreement on this text. For that reason, these decisions have not been adopted,” said the summit’s president, Turkish Environment Minister Murat Kurum, who said he would not offer a new draft. Every one of the nearly 200 countries must agree, so a single objection can block a deal; the talks ran about two days past their scheduled end, the longest such summit on record, and it is the first annual climate summit to close without an overall deal since Copenhagen in 2009.||The EU, Britain, Norway, Panama and island nations such as the Marshall Islands, Tuvalu and Vanuatu objected because the text only “recalled” the 2023 Dubai pledge to transition away from fossil fuels, while Russia said it could accept no fossil-fuel wording at all and Saudi Arabia and other oil producers resisted stronger language. Speaking for the EU, Ireland’s climate minister, Darragh O’Brien, said the talks had been “hijacked by some oil states”; U.N. Secretary-General António Guterres said he was “deeply disappointed.” The United States sent no delegation for the second year in a row.||The failed text would have acknowledged for the first time that warming is on course to pass 1.5 degrees Celsius (2.7 degrees Fahrenheit) and called for at least tripling aid to help poor countries adapt by 2035. More than 90 countries separately signed a voluntary pledge to expand the use of electricity. The open questions go to midyear talks in Bonn, Germany, in June and to next year’s summit in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia; earlier agreements remain in force.'))
body.append('<h3>Russian drones kill at least 15 around Kyiv</h3>')
body.append(P('A Russian drone struck a five-story apartment building overnight Saturday in Dymer, a town about 28 miles north of Kyiv, Ukraine’s capital, killing at least 11 people, regional officials said; a 7-year-old boy was pulled out alive after nearly 14 hours under the rubble. On Sunday evening a drone hit a supermarket in Vyshneve, a suburb southwest of the city, while shoppers were inside, killing three and injuring 15, and a strike on a home in the Bila Tserkva district killed one more. The Kyiv regional governor, Tymur Tkachenko, declared today a day of mourning.||President Volodymyr Zelensky called the attacks “constant Russian terror” and said Russia had fired some 2,700 long-range drones, 1,460 glide bombs and 165 missiles at Ukraine over the past week. A Ukrainian assault regiment said a counteroffensive had retaken 14 villages, about 79 square miles, in northern Kharkiv province near the Russian border; Russia disputes it <i>(unconfirmed)</i>. Russian President Vladimir Putin said Thursday that Russia would not negotiate after Ukraine’s strikes on the bridge to occupied Crimea.'))
body.append('<h3>Russian warship sunk at a Libyan port by sea drones</h3>')
body.append(P('The Merkury, a small Russian navy warship known as a corvette, was hit at dawn Sunday by sea drones, unmanned explosive boats, while docked in Benghazi, the main city of eastern Libya, setting off an explosion and fire, Libyan officials said; satellite images showed it capsized with only its bow above water. The eastern Libyan government, a rival to the U.N.-recognized government in Tripoli, said the ship had stopped to refuel under a cooperation agreement with Russia and that at least four other drones were shot down. Casualty reports vary and have not been confirmed. That evening a sea drone also hit a Malta-flagged cargo ship off Benghazi, injuring crew members who were rescued.||No one has claimed the attack, and Russia had not commented in first reports. The AP, citing unnamed sources, reported that the drones were launched from a Ukrainian-run base in western Libya <i>(single source: AP; not independently confirmed)</i>. Eastern Libya’s prime minister, Osama Hammad, has asked the U.N. for an international investigation. It came a day after drones set a Russian cargo ship ablaze in the Gulf of Finland.'))
body.append('<h3>Trump says he is inclined to resume strikes on Iran after New Year if talks fail</h3>')
body.append(P('President Trump told Fox News, in remarks reported Sunday, that he is inclined to resume major military strikes on Iran after the new year if negotiations, held indirectly with Qatar as go-between, do not produce a permanent agreement keeping Iran from nuclear weapons; he called Iran’s negotiators “professional tap dancers.” No decision has been announced, and Iran’s deputy foreign minister, Kazem Gharibabadi, said the window for diplomacy remains open but Iran is ready for any scenario.||Fighting around the Strait of Hormuz, the narrow passage that carries much of the world’s oil, went on: a British military agency that tracks shipping said two tankers leaving the strait were hit by “unknown projectiles” off Oman on Saturday, and Iran’s navy said tankers struck mines in a “forbidden” zone. Iran’s Revolutionary Guard claimed it hit two U.S. Patriot air-defense radars in Bahrain, home of the U.S. Navy’s Fifth Fleet <i>(unconfirmed by the U.S.)</i>; Bahrain said a civilian industrial site caught fire and two residents were wounded.'))
body.append('<h3>Settler arson in the West Bank as Israeli coalition talks stall</h3>')
body.append(P('Israeli settlers set fire to Palestinian cars and sprayed graffiti such as “Revenge” and “Death to Arabs” in several villages near Ramallah, in the occupied West Bank, early Sunday, residents said; no one was hurt, police opened an investigation and the military condemned the attacks. Gadi Eisenkot, the former army chief who is trying to form Israel’s next government after the Oct. 27 election, called it “Jewish terrorism in every sense of the word.”||Later Sunday, negotiators for Mr. Eisenkot’s party, Yashar, and Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s Likud met for more than four hours on a possible unity government without a breakthrough, and agreed to meet again; a main sticking point is Likud’s demand that the far-right National Security Minister Itamar Ben-Gvir be included. Neither camp holds a majority of the 120-seat parliament, and Mr. Netanyahu remains caretaker prime minister; if no government is formed, Israel would vote again next year.'))
body.append('<h3>In brief</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Indonesia.</b> The search went on Monday at a candy factory in Serang, west of Jakarta, that burned Saturday; rescuers have recovered 26 body bags, and between 34 and 50 workers remain missing, depending on the report.||<b>Nigeria.</b> At least 24 people died when an overcrowded boat carrying mourners to a funeral capsized Friday in Bayelsa State, in the Niger River delta.||<b>Yemen.</b> The Saudi-led coalition said it shot down a drone and a missile fired by Yemen’s Houthi rebels Sunday, including a drone aimed at Riyadh, the Saudi capital.'))

body.append('<h2>Markets &amp; the Farm</h2><p class="note">Figures are as of Friday’s close, November 20, the last trading day. Week changes are measured from the close of Friday, November 13.</p><table class="mk"><tr><th>Index</th><th>Close</th><th>Day</th><th>Week</th></tr><tr><td>S&amp;P 500</td><td class="n">6,910.39</td><td class="n">−265.44 (−3.7%)</td><td class="n">−651.28 (−8.6%)</td></tr><tr><td>Dow Jones Industrials</td><td class="n">48,147.33</td><td class="n">−949.73 (−1.9%)</td><td class="n">−4,200.22 (−8.0%)</td></tr><tr><td>Nasdaq Composite</td><td class="n">22,617.73</td><td class="n">−1,261.47 (−5.3%)</td><td class="n">−2,518.54 (−10.0%)</td></tr></table><p>Wall Street reopens this morning after its roughest week since April 2025, when a selloff in artificial-intelligence and chip stocks, led by a 14 percent drop in Nvidia on Friday, put the S&amp;P 500 and the Dow into a correction and erased the Nasdaq’s gain for the year. Year to date, the S&amp;P 500 is now up just 0.9 percent and the Dow 0.2 percent, while the Nasdaq is down 2.7 percent.</p><p>It is a short week: markets are closed Thursday for Thanksgiving and close early, at noon Jonesboro time, on Friday. Today brings early November readings on business activity and results from Zoom and Hewlett Packard Enterprise after the close; Tuesday, consumer confidence and results from Best Buy, Medtronic, Dell and HP; and Wednesday, a heap of government reports, including October’s PCE inflation index, the Federal Reserve’s preferred price gauge, the second estimate of third-quarter growth and jobless claims, along with results from Deere, the farm-equipment maker.</p>')
body.append('<h3>What analysts are saying</h3>')
body.append('<ul class="picks"><li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: UNH</b> (UnitedHealth Group, the nation’s largest health insurer, which also owns the Optum doctors’-office and pharmacy business). TD Cowen raised it to buy from hold on Friday, named it the firm’s top pick for 2027 and raised its price target to $455 from $402, expecting profit margins at Optum Health to recover and Medicare Advantage plans to earn more. Risk: changes in what the government pays Medicare Advantage plans, a worry that cut the stock about 14 percent over the past three months.</li><li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: PDD</b> (PDD Holdings, the Chinese online retailer behind the Temu shopping app and China’s Pinduoduo). Two firms upgraded it Friday: Macquarie to outperform, with a $106 target, saying Temu shows “clearer signs of approaching profitability,” and BNP Paribas Exane to neutral from underperform. Risk: U.S. trade policy toward Chinese sellers, and questions about how much cash the company returns to shareholders.</li><li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: ABT</b> (Abbott Laboratories, the maker of medical devices, diagnostic tests and nutrition products such as Similac and Ensure). Leerink Partners raised it to outperform on Thursday, with a target of $124, up from $106, expecting medical-technology stocks to do better than the market over the next one to two years. Risk: it is a call on the whole industry more than on Abbott itself.</li><li><b>Analysts turned cautious: GWW and FAST</b> (W.W. Grainger and Fastenal, which sell tools, safety gear and maintenance supplies to businesses). Baird cut both to neutral from outperform on Friday, saying the stocks are priced high while industry profit margins are near their peak and price increases are slowing; it called it a valuation question, “not a company problem.” Risk to the cautious view: both companies are running well, and a steady economy could prove the call early.</li><li><b>Analysts turned cautious: EMN</b> (Eastman Chemical, the Kingsport, Tenn., maker of plastics, fibers and specialty chemicals). JPMorgan cut it to underweight, its sell rating, on Friday, citing flat demand, falling auto production worldwide and a housing market held back by high interest rates. Risk to the cautious view: the firm itself expects strong cash flow next year, which could support the shares.</li></ul>')
body.append('<p class="note">This is information about what analysts said, not advice to buy or sell.</p>')
body.append('<h3>Commodities</h3>')
body.append('<table class="mk split"><tr><th>Commodity</th><th>Close</th><th>Change</th></tr><tr><td>WTI crude (Dec.), per bbl.</td><td class="n">$83.98</td><td class="n">+$0.77</td></tr><tr><td>Brent crude (Jan.), per bbl.</td><td class="n">$92.57</td><td class="n">+$0.59</td></tr><tr><td>Arkansas avg. regular gas (AAA, Nov. 19)</td><td class="n">$3.37</td><td class="n">−18¢ on week</td></tr><tr><td>Jonesboro metro avg. gas (AAA, Nov. 19)</td><td class="n">$3.35</td><td class="n">−13¢ on week</td></tr><tr><td>Live cattle (Dec.), per cwt</td><td class="n">$223.00</td><td class="n">+15¢</td></tr><tr><td>Feeder cattle (Jan.), per cwt</td><td class="n">$335.42</td><td class="n">−12¢</td></tr><tr><td>Lean hogs (Dec.), per cwt</td><td class="n">$52.60</td><td class="n">+17¢</td></tr><tr><td>Corn (Dec.), per bu.</td><td class="n">$4.34¼</td><td class="n">−4½¢</td></tr><tr><td>Soybeans (Jan.), per bu.</td><td class="n">$12.20¾</td><td class="n">+13¼¢</td></tr><tr><td>Wheat, Chicago (Dec.), per bu.</td><td class="n">$5.74¼</td><td class="n">−4¢</td></tr><tr><td>Rough rice (Jan.), per cwt</td><td class="n">$18.65½</td><td class="n">unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Cotton (Dec.), per lb.</td><td class="n">75.47¢</td><td class="n">−0.48¢</td></tr></table>')
